SUMMARY:SFC Education: W-2 vs. 1099
DESCRIPTION:FORMAT: Carousel\nGOAL: Beginner tax education\n\nCAROUSEL: W-2 = generally employee / taxes usually withheld. 1099 = may be contractor/self-employed / taxes may not have been withheld. Different paperwork\, different situation.\n\nIG/FB CAPTION:\nW-2 and 1099 income are not interchangeable\, friend. 😭\n\nA W-2 usually means you worked as an employee and your employer withheld certain taxes from your checks.\n\nA 1099 may mean you earned money as an independent contractor\, freelancer\, gig worker\, or through another type of reportable payment\, and taxes may not have been withheld for you.\n\nThat difference can affect what information you need when it’s time to file.\n\nSo when those forms start arriving\, don’t toss them in the same “I’ll figure it out later” pile. 😂 Keep them together and ask questions if something doesn’t make sense.\n\nSave this for tax season.\n\n@serenityfinancialco\nLess stress. More clarity.\n\nTIKTOK HOOK: W-2 and 1099 are cousins\, not twins. 😂\nTIKTOK CAPTION: Same tax season\, very different paperwork 👀\nCTA: Save this post.\nSTORY: Quiz — Which one usually means employee? W-2 / 1099\nHASHTAGS: #W2 #1099 #TaxTok #TexasTaxPreparer #DFWTaxPreparer #SelfEmployedTaxes
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
